A new NOT NULL column without a DEFAULT, on a table with rows: the plan is
made, and SQLite refuses the copy. Nothing changes, and the error names
the step. (Give it a DEFAULT, let it be NULL, or fill it by the ledger:
column-backfilled.)

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'soup');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, price_kr INTEGER NOT NULL) STRICT;

== plan
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, price_kr INTEGER NOT NULL) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name") SELECT "id", "name" FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"

== error
migrating test.db: table `dish`: its rows, by id, name: NOT NULL constraint failed: dish.price_kr (a NOT NULL column needs a value in every row: a column new to the table takes its DEFAULT, a copied one keeps its NULLs; a step of ledger.sql fills it)
